  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Tuesday)\nIssued at 105 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe main impact focus through the end of the week continues to be\nthe potential for heavy rainfall beginning Wednesday night and\ncontinuing into Friday. A potent upper low is currently tracking \neastward into the Desert Southwest, while Hurricane Polo is making\nits second landfall on the west coast of mainland Mexico. \nTropical moisture associated with the remnants of Polo will be \ninjected into the eastward moving upper low. The true remnants of \nPolo look to then lift northeastward mainly across eastern New \nMexico through the OK/TX Panhandles and into western Kansas \nthrough the day Wednesday. Thus, most of eastern Oklahoma and \nwestern Arkansas look to remain dry through the daylight hours \nWednesday. Some isolated to scattered showers will be possible, \nbut generally coverage is expected to be low. A fairly \nsubstantial mid level dry slot is noted on current water vapor \nimagery wrapping into the upper low and is forecast to track into \nwestern Oklahoma by Wednesday afternoon. This will likely set up a\npseudo dry line moving into central Oklahoma late in the day \nWednesday. At the same time, a secondary plume of tropical Pacific\nmoisture, somewhat tied to the developing Hurricane Rachel, will \nstream northward from Deep South Texas into eastern Oklahoma ahead\nof the advancing upper trough. The result will be a developing \nline of showers and thunderstorms Wednesday evening across central\nOklahoma, advancing eastward into eastern Oklahoma Wednesday \nnight into Thursday morning. The main impact will be heavy \nrainfall as precipitable water values climb to near climatological\nmax values across much of the area. Given the deep saturated \nprofiles, efficient rainfall production is expected and rainfall \nrates of 1 to 2 inches per hour will be common. Also, given the \ntiming of the line being during the overnight hours, severe \nweather chances will stay pretty low as instability is lacking. \nHowever, increasing low level shear will allow there to be a non \nzero tornado risk with the initial line with any stronger \nupdrafts across eastern Oklahoma.\n\nWidespread showers and storms will track eastward through the day\nThursday, while likely weakening as it approaches the OK/AR border\nas the upper low begins to lift northeast of the region. A surface\ncold front will then drop south into northeast Oklahoma Thursday\nnight into Friday. Additional light to moderate showers will be\npossible both ahead of and behind the front on Friday as the\nelongated trough axis remains in the vicinity and moisture wraps\naround the backside of the departing system. Post frontal areas \nacross the northern half of the CWA could see a real taste of Fall\nthrough much of Friday with cloudy and cool conditions, periods \nof light rain, and breezy northerly winds likely. Lingering \nshowers will be possible again on Saturday, mainly across \nsoutheast Oklahoma before a reinforcing cold front brings cool and\ndrier air through the region by Sunday. A multi day period of \nmore seasonable early October conditions will finish out the \nforecast period as surface high pressure settles over the region \nthrough mid week.\n\nTalking about the much anticipated rainfall totals...The trend to\na more progressive upper trough will limit some of the extreme\nrainfall potential across the area. Widespread totals of 1 to 4\ninches are still expected across eastern Oklahoma, with less\nexpected across western Arkansas. Some locations across eastern\nOklahoma could still see locally 6-7 inches when all is said and \ndone. Have decided to continue to hold off on a Flood Watch, in \npart because of the extreme drought conditions noted across the \narea, along with some uncertainty on how much the dry slot might \ninhibit totals. We will continue to monitor the trends for a watch\non subsequent shifts.\n\nBowlan",
